// Deep-dive layer: Leviticus and Numbers. Format as in dd_a.js.
export default {
  2: [
    [3, `The sacrifice you eat`,
      `The “peace offering” (*shelamim*) is the odd one out in Leviticus 1-7: most of the animal is not burned, but eaten by the worshipper’s family and friends in a meal at the sanctuary, with God’s portion (the fat) burned first (3:3-5). Religion here is a table. Mary Douglas, in *Leviticus as Literature* (1999), argued the book is a carefully built ring composition in which the sanctuary is a map of the mountain at Sinai, and meals are the point of contact. The ban on eating fat and blood (3:17) is “a perpetual statute,” and later law restricts where such meals can happen. Paul uses the same logic in 1 Corinthians 10:16-18, comparing the Lord’s table to the altar of Israel.`,
      [[`Leviticus 7:11-18`, `The thanksgiving meal must be eaten the same day: no leftovers.`], [`1 Corinthians 10:16-18`, `Paul on sharing in the altar, applied to the Lord’s Supper.`]],
      [`Why does a book about sacrifice give so much space to a sacrifice that ends in dinner?`, `God’s portion comes first, then the people’s. What does that order teach?`],
      [`predict`, `Before you read chapter 7, predict what rules will govern a shared meal with God: how long may the meat be kept, and who may eat?`]],
    [4, `Sin as pollution, not only debt`,
      `The “sin offering” (*chatta’t*, better “purification offering”) is for unintended wrongs, and its blood is smeared on the altar horns or sprinkled before the veil, depending on who sinned: priest or whole community (bull, blood in the sanctuary), ruler (male goat), ordinary person (female goat or lamb). Jacob Milgrom (Anchor Bible *Leviticus 1-16*, 1991) argued that the more serious the sin, the deeper the pollution reaches into the sanctuary, so the blood cleanses the shrine, not the sinner. On this reading sin is a contaminant that travels. Hebrews 9:22 later says “without the shedding of blood there is no forgiveness,” and Numbers 15:30-31 draws a line at the sin done “with a high hand,” for which no sacrifice exists.`,
      [[`Leviticus 16:16-19`, `On the Day of Atonement the whole sanctuary is purged.`], [`Numbers 15:27-31`, `Unintentional wrongs can be atoned; deliberate defiance cannot.`]],
      [`Why does a book of law require a sacrifice for sins done by mistake?`, `If wrongdoing leaves a residue the community has to clean up, what would count as a modern example?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you think of wrongdoing?`, `As a debt, which must be paid or forgiven.`, `As a stain or contamination, which must be cleansed.`]],
    [5, `The sin of keeping quiet`,
      `Leviticus 5:1 names a sin that is easily missed: a person hears a public curse, has seen or known something, and “does not speak.” That person “bears iniquity.” Silence is guilt. The chapter also addresses careless oaths: someone who swears “rashly… to do evil or to do good” (5:4) is guilty when they realise it, a reminder of Jephthah’s vow in Judges 11. The guilt offering (*asham*) is for sacrilege and wrongs against property, and the person must make restitution plus a fifth of the value (5:16). Jesus’ teaching against oaths (Matthew 5:33-37) can be read against this background: the problem is the casual invocation of God’s name for things we cannot control.`,
      [[`Judges 11:30-40`, `Jephthah’s rash vow, the tragic case the law implicitly warns against.`], [`Matthew 5:33-37`, `Jesus: let your yes be yes.`]],
      [`When has silence been the sin in a situation you know of? What would speaking have cost?`, `Why does the law add a fifth when someone makes restitution?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write a one-paragraph “confession of silence” for a time you knew something and said nothing.`]],
    [6, `Fraud is a sin against God before it is a crime`,
      `Leviticus 6:1-7 starts with a surprising phrase: if someone lies to a neighbour about a deposit, a robbery or a lost item, “he has committed a breach of faith against the LORD.” The offender must restore the property in full, add a fifth, and bring a guilt offering. This puts harm to a neighbour in the same category as harm to the sanctuary. Zacchaeus’ promise in Luke 19:8 to repay four times what he defrauded goes beyond what the law required, which is part of what makes the story land. David in Psalm 51:4 says to God, “against you, you only, have I sinned,” though he has wronged Uriah and Bathsheba: this passage helps make sense of that claim.`,
      [[`Luke 19:1-10`, `Zacchaeus pays four times, far beyond the law’s one-fifth.`], [`Psalm 51:1-4`, `David names God as the one he has wronged.`]],
      [`Why would the law say theft from a neighbour is a breach of faith against God?`, `What does Zacchaeus’ overshoot show about the difference between law and repentance?`],
      [`choose`, `What does Leviticus 6:2 suggest?`, `All social wrongs have a religious dimension, because God guards the community’s trust.`, `The verse extends the sanctuary’s rules to cover commerce, bringing religion to the marketplace.`]],
    [7, `The priests’ pay, and what happens when they steal it`,
      `Leviticus 7 specifies who gets which cut: the breast and right thigh of the peace offering go to the priests (7:31-34). This is their salary, a form of income in a landless caste. The chapter repeats the ban on eating fat and blood, with the penalty of being “cut off” (*karet*). The need for such detail shows up in what happens when it is ignored: 1 Samuel 2:12-17 describes Eli’s sons grabbing meat before the fat was burned, and Malachi 1:6-14 accuses the priests of accepting blind and lame animals. Paul invokes the principle in 1 Corinthians 9:13: those who serve at the altar share in what is offered.`,
      [[`1 Samuel 2:12-17`, `Hophni and Phinehas take their cut by force.`], [`Malachi 1:6-8`, `A prophet’s joke: try offering that blind animal to your governor.`]],
      [`Why would a legal text specify which cuts of meat go to whom?`, `Malachi accuses priests of accepting second-best offerings. What is the modern equivalent of a blind and lame sacrifice?`],
      [`rate`, `How closely should those who work in religious institutions be paid by the people they serve?`, `Not at all`, `Entirely`]],
    [8, `Aaron is consecrated after making the calf`,
      `Leviticus 8 narrates the ordination commanded in Exodus 29, and the sequence is striking: Aaron has just led the golden calf (Exodus 32), and now he is washed, robed and anointed as high priest. The text does not mention the calf. Whether that is mercy or the preservation of two traditions that were never harmonised is debated. In Leviticus 9:7 Moses tells Aaron to make atonement for himself first, and Hebrews 5:1-3 develops that point: the priest “is beset with weakness” and offers for his own sins. The institution is designed with the assumption that the person in it is flawed.`,
      [[`Exodus 32:1-6`, `Aaron makes the calf, in the chapter before the priestly instructions are carried out.`], [`Hebrews 5:1-4`, `The high priest’s weakness as part of his job description.`]],
      [`What does it mean that the high priest is the man who made the calf?`, `Is an institution stronger when its leaders are presented as flawless or when they are shown as weak?`],
      [`choose`, `Why would the text place these chapters side by side?`, `The institution matters more than the person: God works through flawed men.`, `The tension is deliberate: priesthood is shown as both necessary and compromised.`]],
    [11, `Why not pork? Five theories`,
      `Leviticus 11 divides animals into clean and unclean, and the reasons have been argued for centuries. (1) Hygiene: pigs carry parasites (Maimonides, partly). (2) Anomaly: Mary Douglas’ *Purity and Danger* (1966) argued the law bans creatures that fit no clean category, such as a split hoof without cud; in 1999 she revised this to an analogical system tied to the sanctuary. (3) Ecology: Marvin Harris (*Cows, Pigs, Wars and Witches*, 1974) said pigs compete with humans for water and shade in the hill country. (4) Ethics: Jacob Milgrom saw a discipline limiting killing to a small set of domestic animals. (5) Identity: archaeologists note almost no pig bones at highland Israelite sites but plenty at Philistine ones, so the law may codify a boundary. The reason given in the text is holiness: “be holy, for I am holy” (11:44).`,
      [[`Mark 7:18-19`, `“Thus he declared all foods clean.” Mark’s reading of Jesus on dietary law.`], [`Acts 10:9-16`, `Peter’s vision of the sheet: the food rule becomes a rule about Gentiles.`]],
      [`Which of the five theories fits best with the rest of Leviticus, and why?`, `How did Mark and Acts re-read a food law as something else?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the dietary law mainly for?`, `Marking Israel off as a distinct people.`, `Training habits of restraint and reverence for life.`]],
    [12, `After a birth: purification, not penance`,
      `A mother is ritually unclean for seven days after bearing a son and fourteen after a daughter, followed by 33 or 66 days of “blood of purifying” (12:2-5). Then she brings a lamb and a bird, or two birds if she cannot afford a lamb (12:8). The doubling for a girl is unexplained. The “sin offering” here is a purification offering and does not suggest the birth was sinful; Milgrom argued the issue is the flow of blood, which belongs to the borderland between life and death. In Luke 2:22-24, Mary brings two birds, which tells the reader her family could not afford a lamb.`,
      [[`Luke 2:22-24`, `Mary offers the poor person’s alternative.`], [`Leviticus 15:19-30`, `The related laws of menstrual flow.`]],
      [`What does the postpartum offering tell you about the Bible’s view of birth, if it is not about sin?`, `The law makes a provision for those who cannot pay. Why might that matter for a ritual law?`],
      [`predict`, `Before reading, predict what the law will say about a daughter compared with a son, and then notice whether the text explains the difference.`]],
    [13, `“Leprosy” is probably not leprosy`,
      `The Hebrew word *tsara’at* covers a range of skin conditions, plus mould in fabric and in houses (13:47-59; 14:33-53). Hansen’s disease (modern leprosy) is probably not what is described. The priest’s role is diagnostic: he inspects, quarantines and declares clean or unclean; he is not a healer. The person with the condition must tear their clothes, leave their hair loose and cry “Unclean!” (13:45-46), the signs of a mourner, as though the person were already counted among the dead. Miriam’s “leprosy” in Numbers 12:12 is compared to a stillborn child. When Jesus touches a leper in Mark 1:40-45 and then tells him to show himself to the priest, he is both crossing and affirming the law.`,
      [[`Numbers 12:10-15`, `Miriam is shut out of the camp for seven days.`], [`Mark 1:40-45`, `Jesus touches the man before sending him to the priest.`]],
      [`Why would the law treat a skin condition as something like death?`, `What does Jesus’ touch do that the priest’s inspection could not?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Rewrite the priest’s diagnosis (13:2–8) as a modern checklist, and notice what the change does to the tone.`]],
    [14, `Two birds: one dies, one flies`,
      `The cleansing ritual in 14:1-7 uses two birds: one is killed over fresh running water; the other is dipped in its blood and released into open country. The living bird’s release anticipates the scapegoat in Leviticus 16:20-22. The person then undergoes a second ritual: the blood on the ear, thumb and toe (14:14), the same triple application used to ordain priests in Exodus 29:20. A person who has been outside the camp is returned with rites that treat them as part of a priesthood. The chapter ends with the strangest case: mould in a house, which can lead to demolition (14:33-53).`,
      [[`Leviticus 16:20-22`, `The goat sent away on the Day of Atonement.`], [`Exodus 29:20`, `The ordination blood on ear, thumb and toe, the pattern used here.`]],
      [`What does the released bird mean in a ritual of return?`, `Why would a healed outcast be given the same ritual as a priest?`],
      [`predict`, `Before reading, predict what the second bird’s fate will say about what the ritual is trying to enact.`]],
    [15, `A chapter about bodies that mentions God only at the end`,
      `Leviticus 15 treats male and female discharges in parallel (15:2-18; 15:19-30). The uncleanness is not moral: it comes from the body’s ordinary and abnormal flows, and most of it clears with washing and waiting. God appears only in 15:31: the people must be kept from uncleanness “lest they die in their uncleanness by defiling my tabernacle.” The issue is proximity to the sanctuary. In Mark 5:25-34, a woman with a flow of blood touches Jesus, and the direction of contagion reverses: his power goes out, and she is healed. Ezekiel 36:17 uses menstrual impurity as a metaphor for Israel’s sin, a use some find troubling.`,
      [[`Mark 5:25-34`, `A woman touches Jesus; uncleanness does not spread, healing does.`], [`Ezekiel 36:16-17`, `A prophet’s metaphor built on this law.`]],
      [`How would you explain “unclean” to someone who thinks it means “sinful”?`, `In Mark 5, who is contaminated and who is cleansed?`],
      [`choose`, `What is Leviticus 15 mainly about?`, `Keeping the sanctuary safe from contact with death and flux.`, `Marking the body as a place where the boundary of life and death is always at stake.`]],
    [17, `“The life is in the blood”`,
      `Leviticus 17 requires that all slaughter of domestic animals happen at the sanctuary, and bans the eating of blood: “the life of the flesh is in the blood, and I have given it for you on the altar to make atonement for your souls” (17:11). Deuteronomy 12:15-16 later allows slaughter at home as long as the blood is poured out, a clear change from this chapter, presumably because worship has been centralised and travel is impractical. Genesis 9:4 first bans blood after the flood. The “Apostolic Decree” of Acts 15:20, 29, asking Gentile believers to abstain from blood, picks up the same prohibition. Verse 7 mentions offerings to “goat-demons” (*se’irim*), a hint of practices the law is trying to stop.`,
      [[`Deuteronomy 12:15-16`, `Home slaughter allowed: the law revised for a changed situation.`], [`Acts 15:19-21`, `The early church keeps the prohibition on blood for Gentile believers.`]],
      [`Leviticus 17 and Deuteronomy 12 give different rules for slaughter. How does a tradition handle its own revisions?`, `Why does blood carry the idea of life, and why must it be given to God?`],
      [`choose`, `How should we read the difference between Leviticus 17 and Deuteronomy 12?`, `As an adaptation of one law to a new situation: a living tradition.`, `As evidence that the books come from different times and schools.`]],
    [20, `Two lists, one reason: the land will vomit you out`,
      `Leviticus 20 repeats and extends the prohibitions of chapter 18, with penalties: giving children to Molech (20:2-5), cursing parents (20:9), adultery (20:10), various sexual offences (20:11-21), and mediums (20:27). The stated reason is that the land itself will “vomit you out” as it did the nations before you (20:22-23). Scholars debate whether “Molech” was a god or a type of offering (*molk*), and whether child sacrifice was an actual practice; Jeremiah 7:31 and 2 Kings 23:10 imply it was. Traditions differ on which laws still bind: Aquinas and the Reformers separated moral from ceremonial and civil laws; Milgrom argued that many apply only to Israelites in the land. How a tradition makes this division is an argument about its own principles.`,
      [[`Leviticus 18:24-30`, `The same reasoning, the land defiled by the nations’ practices.`], [`Jeremiah 7:30-31`, `Topheth and the burning of children, which God “did not command.”`]],
      [`Which penalties in this chapter does your tradition treat as binding, and on what principle?`, `If the land itself can “vomit out” a people, what does that say about the relation of ethics and ecology?`],
      [`choose`, `How should a reader decide which laws in Leviticus 20 still apply?`, `By a division of moral, civil and ceremonial law.`, `By seeing all of it as covenant law for Israel in the land, reinterpreted by Jesus and Paul for others.`]],
    [21, `A holiness that excludes bodies, and the prophets who reverse it`,
      `Leviticus 21 bars priests with a physical blemish from offering at the altar: the blind, lame, disfigured, crushed in foot or hand, hunchbacked or dwarfed (21:16-23), though they may still eat the holy food. A related rule in Deuteronomy 23:1 excludes men whose testicles are crushed from the assembly. Isaiah 56:3-5 reverses it: the eunuch who holds fast to the covenant will have “a monument and a name better than sons and daughters.” In Acts 8:26-39, an Ethiopian eunuch reads Isaiah and is baptised. The arc runs from exclusion to welcome inside the Bible itself.`,
      [[`Isaiah 56:3-8`, `The eunuch and the foreigner welcomed into the house of prayer.`], [`Acts 8:34-39`, `A eunuch asks, “What prevents me from being baptised?”`]],
      [`Why does the same Bible contain a rule of exclusion and a prophet who reverses it?`, `The blemished priest is barred from the altar but may eat the holy food. What does that distinction teach?`],
      [`choose`, `How should we read the arc from Leviticus 21 to Isaiah 56?`, `A development inside Scripture: later revelation corrects earlier limits.`, `Two different kinds of holiness: priestly purity for the altar and covenant faithfulness for everyone.`]],
    [22, `A blemished gift is not a gift`,
      `Leviticus 22:17-25 lists animals that may not be offered: blind, injured, maimed, with sores. An animal with a limb too long or too short may be a freewill offering but not a vow offering (22:23), a fine distinction. The rule comes with the instruction “you shall not profane my holy name” (22:32). Malachi 1:8 takes this literally as an insult: “Try presenting that to your governor.” The chapter also deals with who may eat the priests’ food: a bought slave may, a hired worker may not (22:10-11), a daughter returning to her father’s house after widowhood may (22:13). Paul’s call in Romans 12:1 to present bodies as “a living sacrifice, holy and acceptable” borrows this vocabulary.`,
      [[`Malachi 1:6-14`, `The prophet’s case against second-rate offerings.`], [`Romans 12:1-2`, `Paul’s living sacrifice, in the language of acceptable gifts.`]],
      [`What counts as a “blemished” gift in your own life: time, attention, money?`, `Why would the text allow some imperfect offerings (freewill) but not others (vows)?`],
      [`rate`, `How often do you give God, or a cause, what costs you little?`, `Rarely`, `Often`]],
    [23, `A calendar as a theology of time`,
      `Leviticus 23 sets out the festivals: Sabbath, Passover and Unleavened Bread, firstfruits, Weeks (*Shavuot*, fifty days later, hence “Pentecost”), Trumpets, the Day of Atonement, and Booths. It adds, in the middle of the harvest festival, an instruction to leave the edges of the field for the poor and the stranger (23:22). The Dead Sea Scrolls show that calendar was a battlefield: the Book of Jubilees and the Qumran community followed a 364-day solar calendar, believing the Jerusalem temple’s lunar-solar one put the festivals on the wrong days. “When” to worship, as well as how, divided Jews. Acts 2:1 sets the coming of the Spirit on Pentecost, and John 7:37 has Jesus speak on the last day of Booths.`,
      [[`Acts 2:1-4`, `The Spirit comes on Shavuot.`], [`John 7:37-39`, `Jesus’ promise of living water on the last day of Booths.`]],
      [`The Qumran community believed the temple’s calendar was wrong. What is at stake in the question of when to worship?`, `Why would the harvest festival include a command to leave grain for the poor?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Design your own “calendar of memory”: four dates in the year and what each remembers.`]],
    [24, `“An eye for an eye” was a limit, not a licence`,
      `The law of retaliation (24:19-20; see Exodus 21:23-25) is often quoted as a call for revenge, but in context it limits it: the penalty must match the injury. Hammurabi’s code (§§196-200) applied talion between equals but let rich offenders pay silver for injuring a commoner. Leviticus 24:22 says, “You shall have the same rule for the sojourner and for the native.” The Talmud (Bava Kamma 83b-84a) reads the law as monetary compensation. Jesus in Matthew 5:38-42 quotes it and then tells his hearers not to retaliate. The chapter also contains a trial of a man with an Egyptian father who cursed God’s name and was stoned.`,
      [[`Matthew 5:38-42`, `Jesus quotes the law and turns it.`], [`Deuteronomy 19:15-21`, `The law requires two witnesses and punishes false witnesses with the penalty they sought.`]],
      [`Why was a rule that limits revenge a social advance?`, `The blasphemer is a man of mixed parentage, and the law applies to him equally. What does that show about equality under the law?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Rewrite “an eye for an eye” as a modern principle of proportion, in a single sentence, then compare it with Jesus’ response.`]],
    [26, `The land finally gets its Sabbaths`,
      `Leviticus 26 sets blessings against curses, with a sevenfold escalation (26:18, 21, 24, 28) like those at the end of Assyrian vassal treaties (Esarhaddon’s Succession Treaty, 672 BCE). The most startling part is the description of exile: “Then the land shall enjoy its Sabbaths as long as it lies desolate” (26:34-35). 2 Chronicles 36:20-21 applies it to the Babylonian exile, “to fulfil the word of the LORD by the mouth of Jeremiah,” counting seventy years for the seventy neglected sabbatical years. Daniel 9 then reinterprets that seventy as seventy weeks of years. The chapter ends with a promise: even in exile, God “will remember the covenant” (26:42-45).`,
      [[`2 Chronicles 36:20-21`, `The exile read as the land’s overdue rest.`], [`Daniel 9:1-2`, `Daniel studies Jeremiah’s seventy years and re-counts them.`]],
      [`What does it mean for a land to “enjoy its Sabbaths”?`, `Is exile in this chapter a punishment, a repair, or both?`],
      [`choose`, `How should we read the exile in Leviticus 26?`, `As punishment for breaking covenant.`, `As the land’s reclaiming of what was owed to it, with a promise that follows.`]],
    [27, `A price list for promises`,
      `Leviticus 27 closes the book with what looks like a rate card. A person “vowed” to the sanctuary can be redeemed: a man aged twenty to sixty at fifty shekels, a woman at thirty, then lower amounts for children and the elderly (27:3-7). Readers often take this as a statement of human worth, but the values are redemption fees for vows, scaled to labour capacity, not a ranking of souls. The chapter also covers fields, firstborn animals, and the tithe (27:30-33). Verses 28-29 treat what is “devoted” (*herem*) as irredeemable, and the terrible case of Jephthah (Judges 11) shows what can follow when a vow is misread. Ecclesiastes 5:4-5 says: better not to vow than to vow and not pay.`,
      [[`Judges 11:30-40`, `A vow that costs a daughter.`], [`Ecclesiastes 5:4-6`, `The sceptic’s advice on vows.`]],
      [`Is the price list a statement about human worth, or about ritual economics? Does the answer matter?`, `What does Leviticus’ closing chapter say about promises: to be taken seriously, or to be avoided?`],
      [`choose`, `What do the valuation numbers show?`, `A hierarchy of worth, however indirectly.`, `A fee schedule for redeeming vows, which says nothing about intrinsic worth.`]]
  ],
  3: [
    [1, `Counting soldiers: the number problem`,
      `Numbers 1 gives 603,550 men of fighting age, the same total as Exodus 38:26, which would imply a population of about two million in the Sinai, which the evidence does not support. In a 1958 article George Mendenhall argued that the Hebrew *elep*, usually “thousand,” can also mean a clan or military unit, so “Reuben, 46,500” could be “46 units, 500 men.” On that reading the total is closer to 5,500 men. Others treat the figures as literary, symbolic or inflated for effect. The point is not to decide the number but to see that the text’s own counting convention is a question for readers: the army is large, and also the book is full of fear.`,
      [[`Exodus 12:37`, `“About 600,000 men on foot” leave Egypt.`], [`Judges 5:8`, `A poem asks if a shield or spear was seen among “forty thousand in Israel,” a number that is hard to take literally.`]],
      [`If *elep* means “unit” in places, what does that do to the story of the exodus?`, `Why does a text preoccupied with counting begin its wilderness narrative with a census?`],
      [`choose`, `How should we read the numbers in Numbers?`, `As literal counts, with the difficulties accepted.`, `As a convention (units, hyperbole, symbolism) that we do not fully understand.`]],
    [2, `The camp as a mandala around the tent`,
      `Numbers 2 arranges the tribes in four groups of three around the tabernacle, each under a standard: Judah, Issachar, Zebulun to the east; Reuben, Simeon, Gad to the south; Ephraim, Manasseh, Benjamin to the west; Dan, Asher, Naphtali to the north. The Levites sit between the tribes and the tent. Egyptian reliefs of Ramesses II’s camp at Kadesh show a square encampment with the royal tent in the middle, which gives the plan a familiar military look. In Ezekiel 48:30-35 and Revelation 21:12-14, the pattern returns as the gates of a restored city, each named for a tribe. The community is arranged around its sanctuary: what is central is what is holy.`,
      [[`Ezekiel 48:30-35`, `The new city has twelve gates named for the tribes.`], [`Revelation 21:12-14`, `The same twelve gates, with apostles’ names on the foundations.`]],
      [`What does it say about a community when its centre is a tent that moves?`, `Judah leads on the east, the side of the sunrise. Why would the plan privilege that position?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Sketch in words the plan of your own community: what stands at the centre, what surrounds it, and who sits on the outer edge?`]],
    [3, `The Levites are a substitute for the firstborn`,
      `Instead of consecrating every firstborn son (as Exodus 13 demands), God takes the Levites (3:12-13, 40-51). The accounting is neat: there are 22,273 firstborn and 22,000 Levites, so the extra 273 are redeemed at five shekels each (3:46-48). But the clan counts do not add up: Gershon 7,500 + Kohath 8,600 + Merari 6,200 is 22,300, not 22,000. The Talmud (Bava Batra 123b) suggests that 300 of the Levites were themselves firstborn and so could not substitute for others. The fact that the text preserves a discrepancy, and ancient readers explained it, shows that numbers were being read closely.`,
      [[`Exodus 13:1-2`, `The original claim: every firstborn is God’s.`], [`Numbers 8:14-19`, `The Levites are given to Aaron and his sons as a gift.`]],
      [`What does a substitute do in this system? Whom does it replace and why?`, `Why would an ancient reader try to explain a numerical gap instead of ignoring it?`],
      [`rate`, `How comfortable are you with the idea of a group dedicated on behalf of everyone else?`, `Uncomfortable`, `Comfortable`]],
    [4, `A job where looking can kill you`,
      `The Kohathites carry the most sacred objects, but only after Aaron’s sons cover them: “they shall not go in to look on the holy things even for a moment, lest they die” (4:20). Touching is forbidden too (4:15). The story of Uzzah in 2 Samuel 6:6-7 shows what that rule means: he steadies the falling ark and is struck dead. The chapter also gives an age for service: thirty to fifty (4:3). Numbers 8:24 says twenty-five, and 1 Chronicles 23:24-27 says twenty. Rabbinic tradition harmonised by taking twenty-five as the age of training and thirty as the age of full service. Law in the Bible adjusts to circumstance.`,
      [[`2 Samuel 6:6-8`, `Uzzah touches the ark and dies; David is angry and afraid.`], [`1 Chronicles 23:24-27`, `David lowers the age of Levite service to twenty.`]],
      [`Why would holiness be dangerous even to those assigned to carry it?`, `The age of service changes across three texts. What does that tell you about how law operates in Scripture?`],
      [`predict`, `Before reading the Uzzah story, predict what you think the author wants us to feel about his death, and then compare with David’s reaction.`]],
    [6, `The oldest biblical text ever found is a blessing on a silver scroll`,
      `The priestly blessing in Numbers 6:24-26 (“The LORD bless you and keep you…”) appears on two tiny silver scrolls from a burial cave at Ketef Hinnom in Jerusalem, discovered in 1979 and read in 2004 by Gabriel Barkay and colleagues. They date to the late seventh or early sixth century BCE, the earliest known quotation of a biblical text. They were worn as amulets. The same chapter has the Nazirite vow (6:1-21): abstain from wine and grapes, leave the hair uncut, and avoid corpses. Samson and Samuel are Nazirites, and Paul pays for four men under a vow in Acts 21:23-26. The vow is open to “a man or a woman,” a rare invitation to lay people.`,
      [[`Judges 13:4-7`, `Samson’s Nazirite status begins before birth.`], [`Acts 21:23-26`, `Paul pays for the shaving of men under a vow.`]],
      [`What is the difference between a blessing and a charm? Does the amulet change your answer?`, `Why would a vow of special devotion be open to any man or woman?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Rewrite the three-part blessing (6:24–26) in your own words, keeping its three-part rise.`]],
    [7, `The longest chapter of the Torah is twelve identical gifts`,
      `Numbers 7 has 89 verses, and most of them repeat the same list twelve times: one silver plate, one bowl, one gold dish of incense, and a set of animals, from each tribal leader on twelve successive days. A reader could skim, but the text refuses to summarise, so every tribe’s gift is acknowledged in full. Nachmanides argued each gift had a different spiritual meaning in the midrashic tradition. The chapter ends with a different note: “When Moses went into the tent of meeting to speak with the LORD, he heard the voice speaking to him from above the mercy seat” (7:89). The ledger gives way to a voice.`,
      [[`Genesis 24:1-67`, `The other long narrative chapter, with a different kind of repetition.`], [`Luke 21:1-4`, `The widow’s small gift is counted with the same weight.`]],
      [`Why repeat the same gift twelve times when a summary would do?`, `What does the repetition do to your attention, and what might it have done to an ancient listener?`],
      [`rate`, `How do you respond to long ritual repetition?`, `I skim`, `I settle in`]],
    [8, `A retirement age for holy work, and a people waved as an offering`,
      `Numbers 8 gives instructions for lighting the lampstand and for cleansing the Levites. They are sprinkled, shave their entire bodies, wash their clothes, and Aaron “waves” them before the LORD as an offering (8:11-15), a gesture usually done with food. They serve from age twenty-five to fifty, and at fifty they retire from the heavy work but “may assist their brothers” (8:23-26). It is the Bible’s clearest retirement rule. The lampstand’s instruction places the lamps to give light “in front of the lampstand” (8:2): the light is for the space of worship. Psalm 134 addresses “servants of the LORD who stand by night in the house of the LORD.”`,
      [[`1 Chronicles 23:24-32`, `David reorganises Levite service and lowers the age.`], [`Psalm 134:1-3`, `A night-watch blessing for the temple servants.`]],
      [`What does it mean to dedicate people as an offering? Who is giving whom?`, `A retirement age is built into the system. What ethical logic lies behind it?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the “wave offering” of the Levites mainly saying?`, `That they belong to God as a gift from the people.`, `That they are removed from the ordinary and re-assigned to the sanctuary.`]],
    [9, `Law made by complaint: the second Passover`,
      `A group of men, unclean from touching a corpse, ask Moses, “Why should we be kept from offering the LORD’s offering at its appointed time?” (9:7). Moses takes it to God, who creates a second Passover for those unable to keep the first (9:9-14), a month later. The pattern recurs: the blasphemer in Leviticus 24, the Sabbath-breaker in Numbers 15:32-36, and the daughters of Zelophehad in Numbers 27 and 36 all trigger new law by case. Hezekiah’s Passover in 2 Chronicles 30 uses the same provision. Law in the Torah is partly a conversation, and petitions are a way of making it.`,
      [[`2 Chronicles 30:1-20`, `Hezekiah celebrates Passover in the second month, and prays for those who were not clean.`], [`Numbers 27:1-11`, `Another law that comes from a petition.`]],
      [`Who in this text is allowed to demand a change in the law, and who is not?`, `What would a religious community look like if it treated petitions as sources of law?`],
      [`predict`, `Before reading Numbers 27, predict what group will make the next petition, and how the response will compare with this one.`]],
    [10, `A “book” of two verses`,
      `Numbers 10:35-36 is the famous ark saying: “Arise, O LORD, and let your enemies be scattered… Return, O LORD, to the ten thousand thousands of Israel.” In Hebrew manuscripts the verses are set off by backward-facing letters (*nun*), and the Talmud (Shabbat 115b-116a) says that they form a book of their own, so the Torah would be seven books. The chapter also contains the silver trumpets (10:1-10), the departure from Sinai after a year, and Moses’ plea to his brother-in-law Hobab to guide them through the desert (10:29-32). Psalm 68:1 and Psalm 132:8 re-use the ark chant.`,
      [[`Psalm 68:1-4`, `The psalm opens with the ark’s war cry.`], [`Joshua 3:3-6`, `The ark leads the people into the Jordan.`]],
      [`Why might scribes mark two verses as a separate book?`, `The ark’s chant is a war cry. How should a reader hold that alongside the “Prince of Peace” texts?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write a modern two-line chant for a community’s departure on a journey, in the ark’s two-part pattern.`]],
    [14, `A generation dies because of a report`,
      `After the spies’ report, the people say, “Would that we had died in the land of Egypt!” and propose choosing a new leader to go back (14:2-4). God threatens to destroy them, and Moses talks him out of it by quoting God’s own self-description from Exodus 34:6-7: “slow to anger and abounding in steadfast love” (14:18). The result is a compromise: forgiveness, but forty years in the desert, one year for each day of the exploration (14:34). The earlier chapter says the spies felt “like grasshoppers in our own eyes, and so we were in their eyes” (13:33), a line about self-perception shaping reality. Hebrews 3:7-19 reads the wilderness generation as a warning.`,
      [[`Exodus 34:6-7`, `The creed Moses quotes back to God.`], [`Hebrews 3:7-19`, `Why that generation did not enter rest.`]],
      [`“We were like grasshoppers in our own eyes, and so we were in their eyes.” When has your own view of yourself become the view others take of you?`, `Moses argues God out of destruction by quoting God’s words. What kind of relationship does that suggest?`],
      [`rate`, `When you read a hard report about a challenge, do you tend to trust the majority or the minority voice?`, `The majority`, `The minority`]],
    [15, `A man gathers sticks on the Sabbath, and what surrounds the story`,
      `Numbers 15 is a mosaic: grain and wine accompany offerings; then a distinction between unintentional sin, which can be atoned, and sin “with a high hand,” which cannot (15:22-31); then the stick-gatherer, who is stoned (15:32-36); then the command to put tassels with a blue cord on garments, “so that you may remember all the commandments… and not follow after your own heart and your own eyes” (15:37-41). The editor’s placement may link the three: the story of the man shows a high-handed sin, and the tassels offer a daily tool against it. The tassel (*tzitzit*) appears in Matthew 23:5, where some lengthen them for show, and in Mark 6:56, where people try to touch the tassel of Jesus’ cloak.`,
      [[`Exodus 31:14-15`, `The Sabbath law on which the case rests.`], [`Mark 6:53-56`, `The sick touch the fringe of Jesus’ garment.`]],
      [`Why do you think the editor placed the Sabbath case between a law about defiance and a law about tassels?`, `What would a modern “tassel” be: a visible reminder that interrupts your own heart and eyes?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Design a personal “tassel”: a visible reminder of one commitment, and say what it should make you do when you see it.`]],
    [16, `Korah’s slogan is democratic: “all the congregation are holy”`,
      `Korah and 250 leaders confront Moses and Aaron: “You have gone too far! For all in the congregation are holy, every one of them” (16:3). It sounds like Exodus 19:6, “a kingdom of priests.” The story’s answer is the test of incense, and the earth swallows the rebels (16:31-33). A coda in Numbers 26:11 notes that “the sons of Korah did not die,” and eleven psalms (42, 44-49, 84-85, 87-88) are attributed to the Korahites, including “I would rather be a doorkeeper in the house of my God” (84:10). The rebel’s line ends up writing hymns. Jude 11 uses Korah as a warning.`,
      [[`Exodus 19:5-6`, `A kingdom of priests: Korah’s line sounds like this.`], [`Psalm 84:1-10`, `A psalm of the sons of Korah, in the temple’s doorway.`]],
      [`Korah’s claim echoes Exodus 19:6. What is the difference between his claim and the Torah’s?`, `The rebel’s descendants write psalms. What does the editor want us to take from that?`],
      [`choose`, `How should we read the story?`, `As a defence of divinely ordered authority against populism.`, `As a story with a real argument on both sides, resolved by God’s act and softened by the coda.`]],
    [17, `A dead stick blossoms`,
      `To settle the question of which tribe is called to the priesthood, each tribe leader lays a staff in the tent overnight. Aaron’s “sprouted, bloomed blossoms, and bore ripe almonds” (17:8). The almond (*shaqed*) is the “watcher,” the same word play found in Jeremiah 1:11-12, where the prophet sees an almond branch as a sign that God is “watching” over his word. The rod is kept “before the testimony” as a warning to rebels (17:10), and Hebrews 9:4 later places it in the ark. Chapter 18 gives the priests no land: “I am your portion and your inheritance” (18:20), a line the Psalms take up in Psalm 16:5 and Psalm 73:26.`,
      [[`Jeremiah 1:11-12`, `The almond branch and the “watching” God.`], [`Psalm 73:25-26`, `“God is the strength of my heart and my portion forever.”`]],
      [`A dead stick blossoms. What kind of authority does that suggest: earned or given?`, `What does it mean to have God as your “portion” if you own no land?`],
      [`rate`, `How much of your security comes from what you own, and how much from something you cannot hold?`, `All owned`, `All unowned`]],
    [19, `The ritual that makes the wise man despair`,
      `The red heifer (19:1-22) is burned and its ashes mixed with water to cleanse those who have touched a corpse. The ritual contains a paradox: it purifies the unclean, but makes the priest who handles it unclean until evening. Jewish tradition called it a *chok*, a statute without a stated reason; the Midrash (Numbers Rabbah 19:3) has Solomon say of it, “I thought I could understand all this, but it is far from me,” quoting Ecclesiastes 7:23. The Mishnah (Parah 3:5) says that only nine red heifers had been prepared from Moses to the destruction of the Second Temple. Hebrews 9:13-14 contrasts the ashes of a heifer with the blood of Christ.`,
      [[`Ecclesiastes 7:23-24`, `The Preacher confesses limits to his wisdom.`], [`Hebrews 9:13-14`, `The ashes of a heifer set against a greater cleansing.`]],
      [`Why would a ritual purify one person and contaminate another?`, `What do you do with a law whose reason cannot be found?`],
      [`choose`, `Which is the better response to a command that seems to have no reason?`, `Obey without a reason, as an act of trust.`, `Keep searching for the reason; a law no one understands cannot form character.`]],
    [21, `A bronze snake the Bible later destroys`,
      `When snakes bite the people, Moses makes a bronze serpent on a pole, and anyone who looks at it lives (21:4-9). Hezekiah smashed it centuries later because people were burning incense to it, calling it Nehushtan (2 Kings 18:4). John 3:14-15 says, “as Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, so must the Son of Man be lifted up.” A copper snake was found in a Midianite shrine at Timna (Beno Rothenberg, 1969), which fits the story’s setting. The chapter cites two sources now lost: “the Book of the Wars of the LORD” (21:14) and a poem about Heshbon (21:27-30). Joshua 10:13 mentions another, the Book of Jashar.`,
      [[`2 Kings 18:4`, `Hezekiah breaks the snake because it had become an idol.`], [`John 3:14-15`, `The lifted serpent as a figure of the lifted Son.`]],
      [`The same object heals and becomes an idol. What makes the difference?`, `The Bible cites books that no longer exist. What does that say about the sources behind the canon?`],
      [`predict`, `Before reading John 3:14–15, predict how a New Testament writer might use a story about a snake on a pole, and then see where the comparison goes.`]],
    [23, `The foreign prophet whose name is on a wall in Jordan`,
      `Balaam is hired to curse Israel and cannot: “How can I curse whom God has not cursed?” (23:8). The Deir ʿAlla inscription, found in Jordan in 1967 and dated to about 800 BCE, mentions “Balaam son of Beor, a seer of the gods,” the only extra-biblical text to name a biblical prophet from this period. In 23:19 he says, “God is not a man, that he should lie, or a son of man, that he should change his mind.” Yet 1 Samuel 15:29 says the same of God, while 15:11 says God regretted making Saul king, and Genesis 6:6 says he regretted making humankind. Balaam is a villain in Numbers 31:16 and Revelation 2:14.`,
      [[`1 Samuel 15:11`, `God “regrets” making Saul king, in tension with Numbers 23:19.`], [`Revelation 2:14`, `Balaam’s name as a warning in a letter to Pergamum.`]],
      [`Balaam blesses here and is blamed elsewhere. Which is he?`, `Numbers 23:19 says God does not change his mind; Genesis 6:6 and 1 Samuel 15:11 say he does. How should a reader hold both?`],
      [`choose`, `How should we resolve the tension about God’s “regret”?`, `Different texts speak of God from different angles: constancy of purpose, flexibility of response.`, `The texts come from different traditions with different views of God.`]],
    [26, `A new generation counted, and one tribe vanishes`,
      `The second census (26:1-65) is taken after the plague at Baal Peor. It shows that the generation of the exodus is gone, except for Caleb and Joshua (26:64-65). The most dramatic change is the tribe of Simeon, down from 59,300 to 22,200. Zimri, who was killed for his union with a Midianite woman at Peor, was a Simeonite (25:14), and Simeon is missing altogether from Moses’ blessing in Deuteronomy 33. The list also quietly notes that “the sons of Korah did not die” (26:11) and introduces Zelophehad, who had only daughters (26:33), the setup for the next chapter.`,
      [[`Numbers 25:6-15`, `The incident at Peor that involves a Simeonite leader.`], [`Deuteronomy 33:6-25`, `Moses’ blessing of the tribes, in which Simeon is absent.`]],
      [`A tribe shrinks by more than half and then disappears from the blessing. What story is the text telling without saying?`, `Why does a census list include a mention of those who did not die?`],
      [`predict`, `Before reading Deuteronomy 33, predict which tribe will be missing from the list, and then check whether you are right.`]],
    [27, `Five women argue the law into being`,
      `The daughters of Zelophehad have no brother, so their father’s inheritance should vanish. They make a case before Moses, Eleazar, the leaders and the whole congregation: “Why should the name of our father be taken away from his clan because he had no son?” (27:4). Moses brings it to God, who says, “The daughters of Zelophehad are right” (27:7) and establishes a general law of inheritance. They are later given land (Joshua 17:3-6). Daughters inheriting was rare in the ancient Near East. The chapter ends with the commissioning of Joshua by the laying on of Moses’ hands (27:18-23), a pattern the New Testament reuses for ordination.`,
      [[`Numbers 36:1-12`, `The men of the clan answer with a counter-case.`], [`Joshua 17:3-6`, `The daughters receive their share, as promised.`]],
      [`What does it take to change a law in this text? Who has the standing to ask?`, `The first chapter wins the women a right; the second limits how they can use it. Is the second a loss?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write the daughters’ plea (27:3–4) in a modern setting, with a different injustice that needs a legal fix.`]],
    [28, `A calendar of continual worship`,
      `Numbers 28 starts with the *tamid*, the “regular” offering: two lambs a day, one at dawn and one at twilight, never missed (28:3-8), followed by the Sabbath, new moon, Passover and Weeks. The same twice-daily rhythm underlies the Mishnah tractate *Tamid*. Daniel 8:11-13 and 9:27 treat the cessation of the *tamid* as the sign of catastrophe, and Josephus (*Jewish War* 6.94) says it stopped on the seventeenth of Tammuz in 70 CE, weeks before the temple fell. Worship that cannot lapse becomes the measure of whether the world is intact.`,
      [[`Exodus 29:38-42`, `The first instruction for the daily lambs.`], [`Daniel 8:11-13`, `The vision in which the daily offering is taken away.`]],
      [`Why would a text build its calendar around a ritual that never changes?`, `What practices in your life would you call a “regular offering”: never skipped, never remarkable?`],
      [`rate`, `How much do you value practices that never change?`, `Little`, `A great deal`]],
    [29, `Seventy bulls for the seventy nations`,
      `The seventh month is crowded: Trumpets, the Day of Atonement, then the eight days of Booths (*Sukkot*), with a sacrifice of bulls that decreases each day: thirteen, twelve, eleven… down to seven (29:12-34). The total is seventy. The Talmud (Sukkah 55b) says these seventy bulls were offered on behalf of the seventy nations of Genesis 10, so Israel’s festival of the harvest was a festival for the world. Zechariah 14:16-19 imagines the nations coming up every year to keep Booths. The sacrifices begin in abundance and wind down to seven, a number of completeness.`,
      [[`Genesis 10:1-32`, `The seventy nations, now the beneficiaries of the offering.`], [`Zechariah 14:16-19`, `The nations go up to Jerusalem to keep the festival.`]],
      [`Why would a festival for Israel be said to atone for all nations?`, `What does a declining sacrifice say about the movement of a festival?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the best reading of the seventy bulls?`, `A later rabbinic insight that makes a hidden universalism visible.`, `A coincidence of numbers that rabbis turned into theology.`]],
    [30, `A father or husband can cancel a woman’s vow, except a widow’s`,
      `Numbers 30 sets rules for vows: a man’s must be kept (30:2), but a young woman’s vow in her father’s house, or a wife’s, can be cancelled if the father or husband objects on the day he hears of it (30:3-15). Silence is consent. A widow or divorced woman’s vow stands (30:9). The chapter reflects a household structure in which a woman’s promises might commit family resources. Jewish law later developed annulment procedures (*hatarat nedarim*), and the Kol Nidre prayer on the Day of Atonement draws on that tradition. Deuteronomy 23:21-23 and Ecclesiastes 5:4-6 stress that a vow is a serious matter.`,
      [[`Deuteronomy 23:21-23`, `“If you make a vow… you shall not delay fulfilling it.”`], [`Matthew 5:33-37`, `Jesus on swearing and vows.`]],
      [`What do the exceptions (widows, divorced women) show about the logic of the law?`, `Is a system of annulment a loophole or a compassion?`],
      [`choose`, `How should we read the chapter’s structure?`, `As a reflection of patriarchal household authority.`, `As protection for a household that had to live with the consequences of any member’s promise.`]],
    [31, `A war of vengeance with a problem for the reader`,
      `Numbers 31 describes a campaign against Midian in which all males and all women who have had sex are killed, and the virgins are kept (31:17-18). Moses is angry that the soldiers spared the women who led Israel to Peor (31:14-16). The totals are striking: of 12,000 soldiers, “not one is missing” (31:49). Yet Midian reappears as a strong oppressor in Judges 6, which makes “annihilation” hard to take literally. Many scholars read the account as idealised tradition rather than a strict record of a campaign. Whatever the reading, the chapter is part of the Bible, and readers across traditions have had to decide what to do with a text that depicts divinely commanded slaughter.`,
      [[`Judges 6:1-6`, `Midian crushes Israel, long after being “destroyed.”`], [`Deuteronomy 20:10-18`, `The war laws that set a different rule for distant and nearby cities.`]],
      [`Midian is back, powerful, in Judges 6. What does that do to the claim in Numbers 31?`, `How does your tradition handle a text that depicts divinely sanctioned slaughter?`],
      [`rate`, `How much weight should a text carry if it describes God commanding violence?`, `Less weight`, `Full weight`]],
    [32, `Two and a half tribes choose the east`,
      `Reuben, Gad and half of Manasseh ask for the land east of the Jordan because it is good for cattle (32:1-5). Moses accuses them of repeating the spies’ failure, discouraging the rest from crossing (32:6-15). They respond by offering to fight in front of their brothers until the land is taken and then return (32:16-27). The agreement is a negotiation, and it draws on language of loyalty. Later, the tribes’ commitment is tested: Judges 5:15-17 mocks Reuben’s hesitation (“great searchings of heart”), and Joshua 22 narrates an altar controversy at the Jordan.`,
      [[`Joshua 22:1-34`, `The eastern tribes build an altar by the Jordan, and war nearly breaks out.`], [`Judges 5:15-17`, `Deborah’s song mocks the tribes that stayed home.`]],
      [`Why might the text allow settlement outside the promised land, and what does it cost?`, `The tribes improvise a promise to avoid Moses’ anger. When is a promise made under pressure binding?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the negotiation?`, `As a pragmatic compromise.`, `As an early crack in the unity of the tribes that the rest of the Bible keeps noticing.`]],
    [33, `A forty-two-stop itinerary that does not match the stories`,
      `Numbers 33 lists the stages of the journey from Egypt to Moab: forty-two stops, many not mentioned in the narrative (Dophkah, Alush, Rissah and others). Deuteronomy 10:6-7 gives a different order of stops and says Aaron died and was buried at Moserah, while Numbers 33:38 says he died on Mount Hor. Ancient Egyptian and Assyrian itineraries list stages in a similar way. Later Jewish mystics read the forty-two stations as steps of a spiritual journey (a tradition in the Zohar and Hasidic teaching). The chapter ends with the command to drive out the inhabitants and destroy their high places, and a warning that those left will be “thorns in your sides” (33:55).`,
      [[`Deuteronomy 10:6-7`, `A second itinerary that disagrees on where Aaron died.`], [`Judges 2:1-5`, `The angel at Bochim: the thorns have come.`]],
      [`Two itineraries disagree about where Aaron died. What does that suggest about how Scripture treats parallel traditions?`, `Why would a text list stops where nothing is said to have happened?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write your own itinerary: six stations of your life with one line each on what happened there and what you learned.`]],
    [34, `The ideal map and the real map`,
      `Numbers 34 gives the borders of Canaan, running from the Dead Sea to the Wadi of Egypt, the Mediterranean, and a northern line toward Lebo-hamath. Scholars (Yohanan Aharoni, among others) have noticed that these borders match the Egyptian province of Canaan in the New Kingdom, a boundary known from Egyptian documents. That is, the Bible’s promised land is described with an imperial map from the period of Egyptian rule. The borders were never fully achieved (Joshua 13:1-7 says “very much land remains”), and a larger ideal, from the river of Egypt to the Euphrates, appears in Genesis 15:18 and 1 Kings 4:21.`,
      [[`Genesis 15:18-21`, `A larger promise from the Nile to the Euphrates.`], [`Joshua 13:1-7`, `The land that remained to be taken.`]],
      [`Why does the Bible hold two different ideal maps?`, `How does it change a promise when it is described as a map?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the chapter’s map?`, `A real description of the land’s boundaries known from history.`, `An ideal outline, aspirational and symbolic.`]],
    [35, `Cities of refuge: a killer is protected until the high priest dies`,
      `Six cities are set aside for someone who kills accidentally to flee the “avenger of blood.” He stays there until the death of the high priest, and then may go home (35:25-28). Murder cannot be ransomed: “you shall accept no ransom for the life of a murderer” (35:31), unlike Hittite and Assyrian law, which allowed blood-money. The chapter also requires two witnesses (35:30) and says blood pollutes the land: “no atonement can be made for the land… except by the blood of the one who shed it” (35:33). Hebrews 6:18 speaks of “we who have fled for refuge” in the language of this law.`,
      [[`Deuteronomy 19:1-13`, `The law of the refuge cities restated with an added third city.`], [`Joshua 20:1-9`, `The six cities designated.`]],
      [`Why would a priest’s death release a killer? What theory of death and guilt is at work?`, `Why does the law refuse ransom for a murderer but protect a manslayer?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the high priest’s death doing in the system?`, `It marks a natural end to the period of exile, as a kind of amnesty.`, `It acts as an atonement that clears the pollution caused by the killing.`]],
    [36, `A law that anticipates its own loophole`,
      `After the daughters of Zelophehad win the right to inherit, the heads of their clan protest: if they marry outside the tribe, their land will pass to another tribe (36:1-4). The law is amended: heiresses must marry within the tribe (36:6-9). The daughters comply (36:10-12). Whether the second ruling undermines the first is a question for readers: it gives the women property but restricts the way they can use it. The book ends not in the land but “in the plains of Moab by the Jordan at Jericho” (36:13), the threshold, setting up Deuteronomy. The Jubilee law (Leviticus 25) lies behind the concern that land should not shift between tribes.`,
      [[`Leviticus 25:8-17`, `The Jubilee returns land to families, which is why inheritance rules matter.`], [`Tobit 6:11-12`, `A later story assumes marriage within one’s clan as the norm.`]],
      [`Does the second ruling undo the first, or complete it?`, `Why does Numbers end on the plains of Moab and not in the land?`],
      [`rate`, `When a victory for justice comes with a limit, do you count it as a win?`, `A loss`, `A win`]]
  ]
};
